+### A frame is on screen 60 to 80 ms after the game presents it (Mac)
+
+- **Symptom:** input feels a few frames late on the Mac though the game holds 60. The log's `game enqueue to target display p50` is 66 to 82 ms in a window, and the per-frame rows (`DM_NVN_TSV`: `@pres_at_ms` − `@call_ms`) show the same in a window and 57 to 61 ms in fullscreen, with `@drawable_ms` at 16.6 there.
+- **Cause:** two queues. In a window the display link calls back about 50 ms before its target display time (`link_latency`), so a frame it takes is three refreshes from the screen, and its own queue holds one more frame when the game runs one ahead. A present thread that takes its own drawables (pacing mode 0, what NXForge does in fullscreen) has no such lead, but the game is paced by its own 60 Hz clock, not the screen's: once it has run a frame ahead (the frames queued at the start, a hitch on the screen's side) the layer's queue of drawables stays full, every `nextDrawable` waits a whole refresh, and the queue is one or two frames of lag that never goes away.
+- **Fix (NXForge, `dm/gfx/nvn/nxforge/low_lag.h`):** while the game has headroom, presents go direct in a window too, and a full queue of drawables is emptied by one frame. Headroom is 180 frames in a row on time with GPU time and the presenting thread's CPU time each at most 0.55 of a vsync; three late frames within 60 end it, and the next try waits 10 s, doubled up to 80 s while the direct runs last under a minute. The trim: 30 presents in a row whose `nextDrawable` waited 4 ms or more drop one present (the picture before it stays one refresh longer, once), at most 6 a minute. In fullscreen, where presents are direct anyway, the same policy decides only the trim. A game without headroom keeps the display link in a window: there the link's queue is what keeps its frames even (below). `DM_LOW_LAG=0`, or the settings window's **Lower input lag**, turns it off; `DM_PRESENT_LINK` and `DM_PRESENT_LATENCY` do too.
+- **Measured** (M4 Pro MacBook Pro, 120 Hz screen, macOS 27.2; game present to on screen, p50):
+
+  | Game and scene | Before | After |
+  |---|---|---|
+  | Nintendo Switch Sports Resort, title, window (GPU 1.5 to 2.9 ms) | 80 to 81 ms | 42 to 51 ms (it follows the phase between the game's clock and the screen's), every on-screen interval 16.7 ms |
+  | The same in fullscreen | 57 to 61 ms | 30 to 36 ms (two runs) |
+  | Bloodstained: Curse of the Moon, window, by `DM_PRESENT_LINK` (GPU 0.5 ms) | 66.5 ms (link) | 34.9 ms (mode 0), 49.8 ms (120 Hz link); every interval 16.67 ms in all three |
+  | Ocarina of Time standing in the field, window, by `DM_PRESENT_LINK` (GPU 16.8 ms, 3,080 draws) | 66 ms at 54.3 fps, 10 % of the intervals over 20 ms (link) | 41 ms at 46.2 fps with 60 % (mode 0): why a game at the edge of its frame keeps the link |
+
+  The switch itself: going direct drops one to three presents within the next second and a half (no on-screen interval over 20 ms in the runs above); going back to the link shows one frame for two refreshes. With `DM_LOW_LAG_TEST=600` (a direct run ends after 600 frames) the lag went 81 → 46 → 65 → 46 → 65 ms over three cycles: the link after a direct run starts with its queue empty.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=<id> t_low_lag && dm/gfx/tests/t_low_lag` (the policy and the trim, simulated).
+- **Not checked:** a game with a frame step (`fstep_tick`) and **Hold frames when late** on, where the switch is reported to the step as a settle (`mtl_fstep_settle`); Vulkan and GL games, which do not come through `nvnQueuePresentTexture` and keep the link; an external or fixed 60 Hz screen.
+- **Found on:** [Nintendo Switch Sports Resort](../../dm/games/04008E001F098000/README.md), [Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md).
+
 ### Frame latency 2 for the display link in fullscreen
 
 - **Symptom:** on a ProMotion built-in screen, only in fullscreen, the frame step went 1 → 2 → 3 vsyncs on frames costing 0.1–0.6 vsyncs (`3 late frames; cost 0.32 vsyncs`) and the game presented 15 frames a second; a window, or the screen set to a fixed 60 Hz, did not do it. The `DM_NVN_TSV` rows show presents shown one 120 Hz refresh late (`@pres_at_ms` − `@link_target_ms` = 8.3 ms) with a skipped tick after each (`@link_gap_ms` 33 ms), and `@link_lead_ms` about 16 ms against about 50 ms in a window.
